(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-13"] = {"13.1":{"s":"DC has a fixed polarity. AC changes direction many times a second (50 times a second in India). AC is easy to step up and down with transformers, so it is used for power supply. This chapter studies how resistors, inductors and capacitors behave with AC."},"13.2":{"s":"An AC generator gives e = e₀ sin ωt, where e₀ is the peak emf and ω is the angular frequency. The current in a circuit connected to it also varies like a sine wave.","f":[{"x":"e = e_0*sin(omega*t)","n":"Emf of an AC generator; e_0 = peak value, omega = angular frequency.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.2, p.288"},{"x":"i = i_0*sin(omega*t + alpha)","n":"Current in the circuit; alpha = phase difference from the emf.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.2, p.288"}]},"13.3":{"s":"The average of AC over a full cycle is zero, so we use other values. The RMS (root mean square) value gives the same heating as that much steady DC: I(rms) = I₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 I₀. Meters show RMS values. 230 V mains is the RMS value; the peak is about 325 V.","f":[{"x":"i_av = (2/pi)*i_0 = 0.637*i_0","n":"Average over HALF a cycle (over a full cycle it is zero).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.3, p.289"},{"x":"i_rms = i_0/sqrt(2) = 0.707*i_0","n":"rms value; the same for the emf: e_rms = e_0/sqrt(2).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.3, p.289"}],"w":["AC meters read rms values; the peak is √2 times larger.","The average of AC over a full cycle is zero; the 0.637 value is for a half cycle."]},"13.4":{"s":"A phasor is a rotating arrow whose length is the peak value and whose angle shows the phase. Drawing voltage and current as phasors turns AC problems into simple vector diagrams. The angle between the two phasors is the phase difference.","w":["Phasors turn anticlockwise; a phasor ahead in the turning direction leads."]},"13.5":{"s":"In a resistor, voltage and current are in phase. In an inductor, current lags voltage by 90°, and the opposition is X(L) = ωL. In a capacitor, current leads voltage by 90°, and X(C) = 1/(ωC). In a series LCR circuit the total opposition is the impedance Z = √(R² + (X(L) − X(C))²).","f":[{"x":"X_L = omega*L = 2*pi*f*L","n":"Inductive reactance (ohm); current lags the voltage by 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.292"},{"x":"X_C = 1/(omega*C) = 1/(2*pi*f*C)","n":"Capacitive reactance (ohm); current leads the voltage by 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.294"},{"x":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2)","n":"Impedance of a series LCR circuit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.295"},{"x":"tan(phi) = (X_L - X_C)/R","n":"Phase angle by which the voltage leads the current.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.296"}],"w":["In DC (f = 0) an inductor has zero reactance and a capacitor infinite reactance: a capacitor blocks DC.","Add the voltages across R, L and C as phasors, not as plain numbers."]},"13.6":{"s":"Average AC power is P = V(rms) I(rms) cos φ, where cos φ is the power factor and φ is the phase angle. A pure inductor or capacitor takes no average power, and the current through it is called wattless current. A pure resistor has power factor 1.","f":[{"x":"P_av = e_rms*i_rms*cos(phi)","n":"Average (true) power; cos(phi) = power factor.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.6, p.298"},{"x":"cos(phi) = R/Z","n":"Power factor of a series LCR circuit; 1 for a pure resistor, 0 for pure L or C.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.6, p.298"}],"w":["A pure inductor or capacitor stores and returns energy each cycle: average power zero (wattless current)."]},"13.7":{"s":"A charged capacitor connected to an inductor makes energy swing back and forth between the electric field and the magnetic field. These are LC oscillations, with natural frequency f = 1/(2π√(LC)). With no resistance they would go on for ever.","f":[{"x":"f = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(L*C))","n":"Natural frequency of LC oscillations.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.7, p.300"}],"w":["Real LC oscillations die out: the coil's resistance turns energy into heat and some is radiated."]},"13.8":{"s":"In a series LCR circuit, the current is largest when X(L) = X(C). This happens at the resonant frequency ω = 1/√(LC). There, the impedance is just R and voltage and current are in phase. A radio is tuned to a station this way.","f":[{"x":"f_r = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(L*C))","n":"Resonant frequency (X_L = X_C), for both series and parallel circuits.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.8, p.301"}],"w":["Series resonance: impedance least, current largest (acceptor). Parallel resonance: impedance largest, current least (rejector)."]},"13.9":{"s":"The Q factor tells how sharp the resonance peak is: Q = ω(r)L/R. A high Q (small R) gives a tall, narrow peak, so the circuit picks one frequency and rejects its neighbours. That is good for tuning.","f":[{"x":"Q = omega_r/(omega_2 - omega_1)","n":"Q factor: resonant frequency over bandwidth; omega_1, omega_2 = where the current is 1/sqrt(2) of its peak (half power).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.9, p.303"}],"w":["Smaller R gives a higher Q and a sharper, more selective resonance."]},"13.10":{"s":"A choke coil is an inductor with very little resistance, used to limit AC current. Because its power factor is almost zero, it reduces the current while wasting almost no energy as heat, unlike a resistor. Tube-light chokes are an example.","f":[{"x":"cos(phi) = R/sqrt(R^2 + omega^2*L^2)","n":"Power factor of a choke; nearly zero because R is very small compared with omega L.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.10, p.303"}],"w":["A choke cuts the current with almost no heat loss; a resistor would waste energy as I²R."]}};
